Fix out-of-bounds read in Rwcrtomb32 on a UTF-8-marked string whose
bytes form a 5- or 6-byte sequence with an out-of-range lead byte.

utf8toucs() decodes the long-withdrawn 5- and 6-byte UTF-8 forms "with
no testing": it neither checks the continuation bytes nor masks the lead
byte to the bits those forms actually carry, so a run of 0xFF bytes is
accepted as a 6-byte sequence whose value overflows 31 bits.  Encoding
that value back with Rwcrtomb32() walks off the end of utf8_table1 and
indexes utf8_table2 one past its last element:

    ==1==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: global-buffer-overflow
    READ of size 4
        #0 Rwcrtomb32      src/main/util.c:1551
        #1 Rf_wcstoutf8    src/main/util.c:1580
        #2 do_chartr       src/main/character.c:1630

Reachable from R whenever a string carrying the UTF-8 mark holds such
bytes -- the mark is not validated, so Encoding<-, readLines(encoding =
"UTF-8") and friends all produce one from untrusted input:

    x <- "J\xff\xff\xff\xff z"
    Encoding(x) <- "UTF-8"
    chartr("a", "b", x)

Released R returns garbage silently; under ASAN it is the overflow above.

Check the 5- and 6-byte forms the way the shorter ones already are:
every continuation byte must be 10xxxxxx, the lead byte contributes only
its low 2 (5-byte) or 1 (6-byte) bits, and 0xFE/0xFF are never lead
bytes.  Invalid input is then rejected with the existing "invalid input
in 'utf8towcs'" error instead of being decoded.  This also corrects the
5-byte branch, which shifted the first two continuation bytes by the
same amount (12) and so never decoded those sequences correctly.  As a
backstop, Rwcrtomb32() now refuses a value above 0x7FFFFFFF rather than
indexing past its tables.

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer.

Upstream status: NOT YET REPORTED to the R Bugzilla as of 2026-10-08.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

